A||||11||Homicidal||Negative Magazine Reviews.....||||11||
Z||000000||Homicidal||09-13-2000||02:39 AM||||Listen guys, I don't know why you guys are suprised to see them.  The Fiero is NOT known as a good, reliable and well performing sports car.  The magazines just shoot the info out because they know the 95% of the public will agree.  Our letters and gripes will change nothing.  The only thing that will change some people's minds is to have them drive one in good condition.  Fiero's have such bad raps mostly now becuase so many are not taken care of, they are assumed crap.  But every person I gave a ride to has apologized the next day for ever dissing my little beauty.  <P>||||reg||11
Z||000001||Oreif||09-13-2000||03:05 AM||||It's not that we are surprised to see them it just seems that they have no idea of any true facts. I have read many articles in many magazines. I just wish one magazine would at least print a fair assesment of the Fiero. It may not be the fastest nor the greatest handling, But for the amount of fun it is to drive it's the best bang for the buck. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Shiner||09-13-2000||04:44 AM||||I think it's a blessing in disguise for us Fiero people.  If everybody knew what great sports cars they were, they wouldn't be so cheap!||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||09-13-2000||07:48 AM||||AutoWeek is on the Fiero's side. Way back in '94 they wrote quite a long two part article detailing the Fiero story. Much of it was transcribed from the book: "Rise and Fall of the American Automobile". If you haven't read this book, I highly suggest you read it, very good, especially the Fiero parts.||||reg||1
Z||000004||HugGRRRFiero||09-13-2000||08:31 AM||||Yep, it's sort of the forgotten American sports car to most of the public.<P>Personally, I think it's pretty cool you can take one of these cars and turn a pile of junk to many into an almost unrecognizable one that turns heads.  Oh and they make for excellent project cars too.  Buy one for a reasonable price and drop in a 3800SC, 350 V8, or even a Northstar!  Add a turbo or two even..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>But, I guess these writers don't seem to recognize that, and just dwell on the fact that 84's had dukes that caught fire.  It ruffles our feathers everytime and makes us want to stand up for the little car for what it means to us.<BR><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">   <BR>   <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||9
Z||000005||Gokart||09-13-2000||09:32 PM||||Everyone is entitled to write the way they feel about any car. Even if they are wrong like the way our Fieros were represented. It wasn't just the negative things said about the Fiero that annoyed me as much as the negative things said about Fiero OWNERS. That was wrong and very unprofessional. I think the author needs to "GET A LIFE"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Gokart_sig_1.gif">  <P>Marc                     87GT<p>[This message has been edited by Gokart (edited 09-13-2000).]||||reg||8
Z||000006||frontal lobe||09-14-2000||02:20 AM||||Where can you get an affordable sporty car now that isn't a total COW.  Most so-called sports cars now weigh about 3500 pounds or MORE.  I don't care if my car has a Chevette front end and a Citation back end.  If GM can take lowly components like that and make them work like they do in the Fiero, compliments to them.  They brought it in at about 2750 pounds.  Way to go.  Plus, it STILL looks good after all these years.  Final bonus, there aren't 10 million of them so you don't see yourself coming and going.  So let the uninformed snobs keep regurgitating what they've read over the last 10+ years.<P>P.S.  I fear no Caprice wagons or Vista Cruisers!!||||reg||1
Z||000007||whamel||09-14-2000||10:30 AM||||What I don't understand sometimes is what brings these current Fiero bashing articles out of the wood-work now?  In reading that last clipping I just don't understand where the guy is going with it.  What made him decide to write about a car that hasn't been produced for years and slam the heck out of it?  Maybe I'll have to go read the whole magazine to see where it fits in, but it just amazes me that he bothered to waste the time to droll out another negative Fiero article this long after production has stopped.  It's not like Pontiac is making noise about doing the Fiero again or anything.  I'm at a loss!<BR>     Hey - did one of you guys in his town cut him off or something?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||ChadMan||09-14-2000||01:06 PM||||Good point on the weight thing, Lobe.  Remember how all the car mags ragged on the Fiero for being "400 lbs too heavy" at 2500 lbs?  I just read a test of four 2-seat roadsters in Road & Track - the lightest was the Honda S2000 at 2700 lbs.  The heaviest was the Audi TT at more than 3400 lbs.<P>My mother's '78 Caprice only weighs 500 lbs more than the TT!  Yet all the car rags think that little German butt-suppository-on-wheels is the greatest thing since the Miata.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Old Lar||09-14-2000||03:04 PM||||I think along whamel lines.  Why write an article bashing a car that has been buried by GM for 12 years?  What's the point?  In reading the article, the author probably wasn't old enough to own a Fiero in it's hay day and is writing from urban legend stories rather than any actual experience.||||reg||5
Z||000010||Wannabe||09-14-2000||10:59 PM||||As a newcomer to Fieroland I can say for certain that these cars are under rated. I spent two years racing an Eagle Talon TSi. Early this year, I decided to switch to a Fiero...boy did I get abuse. Unfortunately, I've only had one chance to put the Fiero on the track this year, but I must say, in spite  of a severly worn suspension and shocks, all the car needed were some good tires to give unbelievable grip. I'm sure that the Fiero with street tires and junky suspension had more grip than my Talon did with R compound tires. I can't wait to get it fixed up and surprise some people in their "sports cars" next season.||||reg||1
Z||000011||frontal lobe||09-15-2000||11:04 AM||||Chadman, LOL.  When I was in pharmacy school (I went to pharmacy school before going to medical school), we had to hand-make suppositories.  Now that you mention it, in retrospect they looked a lot like Audi TT's!||||reg||1
